 line $y=3x.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>x+1,\\,y<3x.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\nx+1<y<3x.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=3x$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx+1<y\\le 3x\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x+1<y\\le 3x\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287728 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181692" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287728 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287728 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/70/7021cfd590d7e090128061d42c140ab81633f20e8cd1bff60f6252bc8e9aa882.png)\n\nUse set builder notation to describe the shaded region given in the diagram above:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,-{y}^{2}\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287728 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b2547003-ad79-57a3-97bf-c8a4091bfc89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287728 #edb/ref 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *to the right* of the curve $x=-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>-{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $x=-{y}^{2}$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\ge -{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge -{y}^{2}\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287766 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287766 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287766 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287766 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287766 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181806" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287766 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287766 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a6/a659e2d14a645036488833cd920e6bb3e3d7a7716724dba63814be6df4066fe9.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\,{{field-1}}\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,6,\\,-1\\,{{field-3}}\\,y\\,{{field-4}}\\,4\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287766 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c447c7d2-83cd-503b-b04b-0c9b2bcb0661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287766 #edb/ref 1074 "A point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3<x<6,\\,-1<y<4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we also need to *include* the left edge ($x=3$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbol to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\le x<6,\\,-1<y<4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\le x<6,\\,-1<y<4\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287785 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287528 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287785 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287664 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287785 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181988" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287785 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287785 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/34/34f5bb40fdcd4f71967ef2b1cc15328cf8d4d02cdd54b19a406273fae6c88ad4.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,y\\,{{field-2}}\\,-x-1\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287785 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dfc3e0f0-9ab6-551c-8d47-c8fca0b2295d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287785 #edb/ref 1074 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x-1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x-1\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny>-x-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,y>-x-1\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287790 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287790 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287790 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182013" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287790 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287790 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/77/7703b8c621b268a9fcbb412cd3a0617045145d475024dc91cecfa09a18fc8712.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,-2\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287790 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "49192157-aea0-5727-927f-dcfccbbb5607" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287790 #edb/ref 1074 "The points in our set include all those that lie *right* the line $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\nx>-2\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $x=-2$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\nx\\ge -2\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,x\\ge -2\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374394 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374394 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374394 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374394 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186374404 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374394 #edb/ref 1069 "q-181159" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374394 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374394 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/99/993cfa437e54f390237b85f3d7ee8a76b626d65431908fe039e0046ba0a33bb1.png)\n\nDescribe the shaded region shown above using set-builder notation.\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\,{{field-1}}\\,x\\,{{field-2}}\\,6,\\,1\\,{{field-3}}\\,y\\,{{field-4}}\\,4\\}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374394 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d62b961-39d3-5633-b650-62ddc2233d7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374394 #edb/ref 1074 "A point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the rectangle if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3<x<6,\\,1<y<4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we also need to\n\n- *include* the left edge ($x=3$), and\n\n- *include* the bottom edge ($y=1$).\n\nTherefore, we change the corresponding inequality symbols to non-strict, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\le x<6,\\,1\\le y<4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since *every* real value in these intervals is included, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\le x<6,\\,1\\le y<4\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374395 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186374396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186374395 #edb/ref 1075 #ed